% holding-blocked: Teacher to check against the plate (p. 64, scan p. 88): bars 3-4 and 7-8 (rhythm), the F fermata notes, the five-note run in bar 2, and the lyric placing; decide whether the irregular bars 3-4 should be re-barred or sung freely as a phonogram transcription.
X:1
T:Lalishrita
T:Tamil lullaby, from a phonogram
C:traditional (Tamil Nadu)
O:India, Tamil Nadu (Srirangam)
R:lullaby
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 155, p. 64
N:Adagio, crotchet 70, taken from a phonogram, so the book writes triplets, grace-like runs, fermatas and two "rall." marks, and the bars are not all of a length. No tonic is marked; the tune circles G with E below and c above and ends on C, and there is no signature, so it is given in C with a drone on C (G would also serve: try both). The words are Fox Strangways's singing translation; stanzas 2 and 3 are below as N: lines because their syllables do not fall under the same notes.
N:HOW READ (2026-11-09): the four staves were cropped at 600, 1200, 1600 and 2400 dpi (scratch crops m155-*, p155-*, q155-*, f155-*, g155-*), every head measured against the staff lines by a detector and then eyed in the 2400-dpi crops; two passes over every bar. Fermata notes in bars 4, 6 and 7 measured between the 4th and 5th lines (F), not G as the first sketch had; "eyes" and "Here" (bar 2) measured G, not A.
N:UNCERTAIN: bar 2, the five-note run under "my" is beamed with a 3 under it (written here as five notes in the time of an eighth); bar 3 is half an eighth over and bar 4 is short (the dotted c may be a dotted quarter); in bar 5 the middle note of the second triplet (B) was read by eye only; bar 7 the quarter on "my" and the pairing of the 16ths; the final two notes are dotted quarters joined by a slur, written here as a tie.
M:6/8
L:1/8
Q:1/4=70
K:C
